# When the Agent Is the Vulnerability
## How Google's "Intended Behavior" determination answered a fully demonstrated agent-hijacking and secret-exfiltration class in Gemini Canvas — and why that answer is itself the reason this write-up is published

**Author:** Dr. Alaaeddine Alrimawi, independent security researcher
**Version:** v4 (2026-10-24) — publication copy
**Safety envelope:** no operational payloads, no copycat-ready mechanics, no endpoint URLs of the researcher's collection infrastructure, no canary values (redacted shapes only). All exfiltrated values were synthetic test canaries created by the researcher. All testing was performed on the researcher's own accounts, documents, and repositories.

---

## 0. Executive summary

Between August and October 2026 I reported to Google's Vulnerability Reward Program a class of indirect prompt-injection attacks in which a single poisoned document in Google Drive — opened with one innocent user prompt — gave an attacker full steering control of Google's Gemini 3.1 Pro agent (Canvas / Drive). The agent bypassed intent filters, scanned the user's files, planted weaponized artifacts, harvested secret-shaped values (Google, AWS, and Stripe key formats, a 39-character token, and an ownership stamp), and transmitted them out through Google's own fetching infrastructure. The entire chain is captured on video, in agent-signed attestations, and in network records held in four separate systems.

Google's final answer on this report, delivered after four mutually incompatible closure rationales, was **"Won't Fix — Intended Behavior"**, accompanied by a Code-of-Conduct warning to me, an accusation that I was "using AI to speculate about security issues", a threat of program ban if I escalated, and a declaration that no further replies would be read. (A parallel report of mine on Google's Jules agent, submitted the same night, met the same endpoint after its own reopen-reclose cycle; it is outside the scope of this write-up.)

Coordinated disclosure exists so that a vendor can ship a fix before publication. A vendor that formally declares there is nothing to fix has dissolved the very premise of the waiting period. My commitment (quoted verbatim from my submission of Oct 5: *"I will not publish anything before I have coordinated a fix timeline with your team"*) was conditional on such a timeline existing. On Oct 12, 2026 I formally requested public-disclosure approval for both reports. Google answered on Oct 14, procedurally: the decision on what and when to disclose is mine, the date is at my discretion, and they asked to be kept informed of my timeline — while offering no fix engagement and leaving the "Intended" determination standing. That same night I gave formal notice of publication on October 23; the exact publication document and its final window, October 23–26, were delivered to the vendor on October 21. As of publication, none of this has produced any fix engagement. The coordination record now stands at the vendor's written acknowledgment of the delivered document — "Thanks!", October 22 — offered without objection.

